WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.896
- I think people need to get,

00:00:01.896 --> 00:00:02.984
sit down and clarify

00:00:02.984 --> 00:00:05.404
what experiences are you really
looking for in your life?

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:07.340
So a lot of people now, "Well,
I want to have a clear head."

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.677
Go "Om." or drink or
something to clear your head.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.510
- Yeah.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:12.401
- As opposed to, wait a minute.

00:00:12.401 --> 00:00:15.081
Why is this on my mind
and what do I need to do

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.039
to get it off my mind?

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:19.890
You need to make action
decisions when you're smart,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.408
so that when you're dumb
you do smart things.

00:00:22.408 --> 00:00:23.939
- He's a productivity consultant

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:26.522
best known for creating
the time management system

00:00:26.522 --> 00:00:28.444
known as Getting Things Done.

00:00:28.444 --> 00:00:31.506
His company focuses on
productivity action management

00:00:31.506 --> 00:00:32.844
and executive coaching.

00:00:32.844 --> 00:00:35.081
Time magazine called
his best-selling book,

00:00:35.081 --> 00:00:36.122
Getting Things Done,

00:00:36.122 --> 00:00:39.139
the defining self-help
business book of its time.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.865
He's David Allen and here's my take

00:00:40.865 --> 00:00:42.771
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:42.771 --> 00:00:44.540
Rule number four is my personal favorite

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.354
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:46.354 --> 00:00:47.726
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:47.726 --> 00:00:49.361
And as always, if David says something

00:00:49.361 --> 00:00:50.677
that really inspires you,

00:00:50.677 --> 00:00:52.304
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:52.304 --> 00:00:53.337
and put quotes around it

00:00:53.337 --> 00:00:55.259
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:55.259 --> 00:00:56.092
Enjoy!

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.947
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:01:09.339 --> 00:01:11.096
- I think people need to get,

00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:11.929
sit down and clarify

00:01:11.929 --> 00:01:15.837
what experiences are you really
looking for in your life?

00:01:15.837 --> 00:01:17.132
And that doesn't necessarily,

00:01:17.132 --> 00:01:18.852
it can include lots of money

00:01:18.852 --> 00:01:19.860
but it can also include

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.159
what do you want to
experience with that money?

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:24.725
A sense of freedom? A sense of service?

00:01:24.725 --> 00:01:27.048
A sense of providing value to the world?

00:01:27.048 --> 00:01:29.215
A sense of self-confidence

00:01:30.214 --> 00:01:31.924
and self-efficacy?

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:34.344
What is it that you're after?

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:36.994
So there's, in my own explorations

00:01:36.994 --> 00:01:40.027
in the whole personal growth
and self-awareness game,

00:01:40.027 --> 00:01:42.825
for years I've been involved
in that pretty seriously.

00:01:42.825 --> 00:01:44.644
There's an exercise, let's
say, well what do you want?

00:01:44.644 --> 00:01:45.990
I want a red Porsche. Great.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.876
What experience do you think
that's going to give you?

00:01:48.876 --> 00:01:51.194
Freedom and a sense of fun, whatever.

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:52.921
And then the people get the red Porsche

00:01:52.921 --> 00:01:55.974
and they're worried about
somebody going to dent it

00:01:55.974 --> 00:01:58.288
and they can't afford the payments on it.

00:01:58.288 --> 00:02:00.636
They didn't really get the
experience they were looking for.

00:02:00.636 --> 00:02:02.152
What happened?

00:02:02.152 --> 00:02:03.715
They got the symbol

00:02:03.715 --> 00:02:05.586
but they didn't get the inner experience.

00:02:05.586 --> 00:02:07.677
But if you want the
experience of freedom and fun,

00:02:07.677 --> 00:02:09.153
you can have that this afternoon.

00:02:09.153 --> 00:02:10.744
Doesn't mean you shouldn't
have the red Porsche

00:02:10.744 --> 00:02:12.436
but it just means that
if you start to focus

00:02:12.436 --> 00:02:13.930
on the inner experience

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.291
what would climbing the
corporate ladder give you

00:02:16.291 --> 00:02:17.446
as an inner experience?

00:02:17.446 --> 00:02:18.803
Why do you want that?

00:02:18.803 --> 00:02:20.837
What's important to you about that?

00:02:20.837 --> 00:02:24.754
And it could be I want
to provide for my family

00:02:26.138 --> 00:02:27.443
for after I'm gone.

00:02:27.443 --> 00:02:29.246
So there's a sense of building wealth

00:02:29.246 --> 00:02:31.469
or building net worth or building assets

00:02:31.469 --> 00:02:34.079
for the people around
you that matter to you.

00:02:34.079 --> 00:02:35.567
There's no right or wrong

00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:37.036
about any of your answers to this

00:02:37.036 --> 00:02:38.423
but I think getting clearer about

00:02:38.423 --> 00:02:40.693
whether you want to do that or
whether you want to get there

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.567
because you want to be able to impact

00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:44.954
a much larger segment of
the world for doing good

00:02:44.954 --> 00:02:47.330
or taking it over, I don't know,

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.614
for whatever you want.

00:02:48.614 --> 00:02:52.100
But I think that's the key
is being able to externalize

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.469
and start to become objective

00:02:53.469 --> 00:02:55.819
about well what is it that you're after?

00:02:55.819 --> 00:02:58.189
And so just crafting your own ideal scene

00:02:58.189 --> 00:02:59.802
not only of the things you want

00:02:59.802 --> 00:03:01.471
which is fine don't stop that.

00:03:01.471 --> 00:03:03.776
Don't feel embarrassed that
you want a red Porsche.

00:03:03.776 --> 00:03:06.000
So there's nothing wrong with any of that

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.250
material stuff that you might want

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.083
but it's also true,

00:03:08.083 --> 00:03:09.308
that's a real good idea

00:03:09.308 --> 00:03:12.159
to just say, let me just lay that all out

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:16.324
and then use that as a
map just to then guide you

00:03:16.324 --> 00:03:18.589
in terms of your
intuitive decision-making.

00:03:18.589 --> 00:03:20.423
I don't think you have
to work at it that hard

00:03:20.423 --> 00:03:22.210
but I think you do need
to be conscious about it

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.320
and validate it in some part of yourself.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.252
Most to-do lists are incomplete lists

00:03:26.252 --> 00:03:28.751
of still unclear things by the way.

00:03:28.751 --> 00:03:31.555
What you see on to-do
lists are things like

00:03:31.555 --> 00:03:32.388
budget,

00:03:34.288 --> 00:03:35.121
mom,

00:03:37.361 --> 00:03:38.194
dog,

00:03:39.791 --> 00:03:41.124
babysitter.

00:03:41.124 --> 00:03:43.340
Well I understand this sort of
probably indicates something,

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.035
that's something you've
got to do something about

00:03:46.035 --> 00:03:48.368
but what exactly is the work

00:03:49.518 --> 00:03:51.580
you need to do?

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.304
And that's going to be the
second thing you need to do,

00:03:53.304 --> 00:03:54.808
is to take those things
that you've identified

00:03:54.808 --> 00:03:56.070
that are yanking your chain

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.624
and you need to make some
really specific decisions

00:03:58.624 --> 00:04:00.597
about what does the work involve

00:04:00.597 --> 00:04:02.377
and there's two key
questions you need to ask

00:04:02.377 --> 00:04:03.922
about everything on your list.

00:04:03.922 --> 00:04:06.162
What outcome am I committed to finish

00:04:06.162 --> 00:04:08.585
so that you can define
that target out there.

00:04:08.585 --> 00:04:10.408
What's the project about the budget,

00:04:10.408 --> 00:04:12.117
about the dog, about the babysitter,

00:04:12.117 --> 00:04:13.424
and identify those.

00:04:13.424 --> 00:04:15.472
And then you need to ask yourself

00:04:15.472 --> 00:04:17.789
what's the very next action
step that I need to take

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:18.716
to move forward on that

00:04:18.716 --> 00:04:20.209
if I were going to move on it.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:21.460
Outcome and actions,

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.530
zeros and ones of productive behavior.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.655
What are we trying to accomplish?

00:04:24.655 --> 00:04:26.602
How do we allocate
resources to make it happen?

00:04:26.602 --> 00:04:28.664
But you need to apply
that very specifically

00:04:28.664 --> 00:04:30.334
if you want to be appropriately engaged

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:32.431
with anything yanking
your chain out there.

00:04:32.431 --> 00:04:34.890
The late great Peter Drucker,
you folks here know that,

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.373
would tell every one of
us as knowledge workers

00:04:37.373 --> 00:04:39.864
the toughest admonition,

00:04:39.864 --> 00:04:43.625
your toughest work is
defining what your work is.

00:04:43.625 --> 00:04:45.281
He'd put it in broad terms,

00:04:45.281 --> 00:04:47.965
this is very specifically
what he's talking about.

00:04:47.965 --> 00:04:49.927
What is the work embedded in that

00:04:49.927 --> 00:04:51.293
and getting very clear about that

00:04:51.293 --> 00:04:52.717
and I guarantee you if you sat down

00:04:52.717 --> 00:04:54.349
and take at least a few of
the things off your list

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:55.298
and make these decisions,

00:04:55.298 --> 00:04:56.351
what's the project?

00:04:56.351 --> 00:04:57.184
Write it down.

00:04:57.184 --> 00:04:58.017
What's the action step?

00:04:58.017 --> 00:04:58.850
Write it down.

00:04:58.850 --> 00:04:59.775
You will feel exponentially

00:04:59.775 --> 00:05:01.448
more appropriately
engaged with your world.

00:05:01.448 --> 00:05:02.911
Never seen an exception to that.

00:05:02.911 --> 00:05:04.925
- [Interviewer] Tell us when
you were just starting out,

00:05:04.925 --> 00:05:06.543
now you're all big and successful,

00:05:06.543 --> 00:05:08.639
back way long time ago in the day

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:10.538
when you were just first starting out,

00:05:10.538 --> 00:05:12.933
what were your most successful
marketing strategies?

00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:14.807
- Well I think if we're,

00:05:14.807 --> 00:05:16.438
what we're talking about really

00:05:16.438 --> 00:05:18.195
is building a business off of the brand

00:05:18.195 --> 00:05:19.028
of building a business

00:05:19.028 --> 00:05:21.293
off the Getting Things
Done model and methodology.

00:05:21.293 --> 00:05:22.758
That actually developed many many years

00:05:22.758 --> 00:05:26.071
after I was doing the work
in consulting and coaching

00:05:26.071 --> 00:05:27.769
but I didn't market myself then.

00:05:27.769 --> 00:05:29.434
Marketing was called pick up the phone.

00:05:29.434 --> 00:05:32.204
It was all referrals whatever

00:05:32.204 --> 00:05:34.485
but even back then I have to say,

00:05:34.485 --> 00:05:36.676
job one called network engage

00:05:36.676 --> 00:05:39.336
like don't turn down any
opportunity to talk to anybody

00:05:39.336 --> 00:05:40.753
about what you're doing.

00:05:40.753 --> 00:05:43.800
As long as they're positively inclined,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.263
so if they're trying to talk to you,

00:05:46.263 --> 00:05:47.967
beat you up or run you down

00:05:47.967 --> 00:05:49.826
or prove that they're better than you

00:05:49.826 --> 00:05:51.285
that's probably not worth while

00:05:51.285 --> 00:05:54.723
but most people are
often gracious about that

00:05:54.723 --> 00:05:56.483
and so you got to ask,

00:05:56.483 --> 00:05:58.200
you've got to put yourself out.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.417
So that's what I've done.

00:05:59.417 --> 00:06:00.955
I've continued to do that.

00:06:00.955 --> 00:06:03.387
As the book came out
then I had opportunities,

00:06:03.387 --> 00:06:05.711
people would, I got press on that,

00:06:05.711 --> 00:06:07.828
that the book publisher produced,

00:06:07.828 --> 00:06:11.057
but I took advantage of
every single podcast,

00:06:11.057 --> 00:06:13.244
every single interview,
every single like this.

00:06:13.244 --> 00:06:14.908
Like you, Stephanie, meeting you,

00:06:14.908 --> 00:06:15.741
saying "Hey David,

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:16.574
"do you want to talk to people?"

00:06:16.574 --> 00:06:18.614
I go, "Sure, I'll talk to people."

00:06:18.614 --> 00:06:22.947
So I'm not actually a
naturally extroverted person,

00:06:22.947 --> 00:06:25.476
it actually is something
I do need to focus on.

00:06:25.476 --> 00:06:28.467
And it's not like I get
all my juice out of that.

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:31.413
So it is a consciously done thing

00:06:31.413 --> 00:06:33.630
and I think out of hundreds and hundreds

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:35.699
and hundreds of interview requests

00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:38.527
and would you write an
article about or whatever.

00:06:38.527 --> 00:06:40.339
I think I've only turned down one

00:06:40.339 --> 00:06:42.270
that just seemed to be a bit sleazy.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.596
My wife is my chief executive assistant.

00:06:45.596 --> 00:06:48.173
She provides a little
bit of a filter for me

00:06:48.173 --> 00:06:49.089
about those kind of things

00:06:49.089 --> 00:06:50.344
but I take advantage of all that.

00:06:50.344 --> 00:06:51.267
So that's job one.

00:06:51.267 --> 00:06:53.224
- [Interviewer] Wow.
Okay, so just saying yes

00:06:53.224 --> 00:06:54.182
to every opportunity.

00:06:54.182 --> 00:06:56.747
- Saying yes to every opportunity

00:06:56.747 --> 00:06:59.664
and also just in terms of my stuff,

00:07:00.642 --> 00:07:04.857
many times, much like
what Mr. Ford used to say,

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:06.804
he never, he never,

00:07:06.804 --> 00:07:09.340
he learned what he knew
by talking to people

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:10.630
'cause he said he had a BS meter

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:11.826
that would go off inside of him

00:07:11.826 --> 00:07:13.466
if he was talking to people.

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:14.309
That's where he learned.

00:07:14.309 --> 00:07:16.322
And I'm pretty much the same way.

00:07:16.322 --> 00:07:18.827
So it's not only a one way street.

00:07:18.827 --> 00:07:20.297
meaning you're helping
other people out there

00:07:20.297 --> 00:07:22.297
but I also matured a lot of my thinking

00:07:22.297 --> 00:07:24.472
about what I was doing
and how to express it

00:07:24.472 --> 00:07:25.984
by doing that.

00:07:25.984 --> 00:07:29.227
- You're a guru of time management
and getting things done.

00:07:29.227 --> 00:07:30.923
So give us the lay of the land

00:07:30.923 --> 00:07:32.395
and then we'll do a little deep dive.

00:07:32.395 --> 00:07:33.228
- You ready?

00:07:33.228 --> 00:07:34.404
- Yes, I'm ready for this.

00:07:34.404 --> 00:07:38.555
- Your head is for having
idea is not for holding it.

00:07:38.555 --> 00:07:39.388
That's it.

00:07:39.388 --> 00:07:40.256
- That's it?

00:07:40.256 --> 00:07:41.798
- If you get that,

00:07:41.798 --> 00:07:46.425
you now have the key to keeping
creative space in your brain

00:07:46.425 --> 00:07:48.031
to be able to have a lot more room

00:07:48.031 --> 00:07:48.985
to be a lot more creative,

00:07:48.985 --> 00:07:51.077
have a lot more fun,
fulfill your life a lot,

00:07:51.077 --> 00:07:52.160
a lot better.

00:07:53.213 --> 00:07:54.429
Most people are walking around,

00:07:54.429 --> 00:07:57.284
most 99.9999% of people
are still walking around

00:07:57.284 --> 00:07:58.779
with the constipated psyche,

00:07:58.779 --> 00:08:00.970
with all the stuff
they're trying to manage,

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.603
keep track of, remember,

00:08:02.603 --> 00:08:04.970
remind themselves about or whatever.

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:07.755
And that is so suboptimal.

00:08:07.755 --> 00:08:11.028
- My mouth is open because
I just, I just got it,

00:08:11.028 --> 00:08:14.481
in that one sentence how profound that is.

00:08:14.481 --> 00:08:16.643
And I think about what you want to say,

00:08:16.643 --> 00:08:17.923
basically say it again

00:08:17.923 --> 00:08:18.930
so that the world--

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:20.249
- Your head's for having
ideas, not for holding 'em.

00:08:20.249 --> 00:08:22.561
- Your head is for having
ideas, not for holding them.

00:08:22.561 --> 00:08:23.739
I'm guessing that's getting retweeted

00:08:23.739 --> 00:08:24.690
around the world right now.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:27.200
- That I need cat food should
pop into your head once.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.461
If it pops in twice,

00:08:28.461 --> 00:08:31.944
you're not handling your
appropriate engagement

00:08:31.944 --> 00:08:33.823
with your cat or its food.

00:08:33.823 --> 00:08:34.656
(laughing)

00:08:34.656 --> 00:08:36.297
Right? I mean, why have an idea twice?

00:08:36.297 --> 00:08:37.680
It's just the reason you do

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.297
and the reason things pop into
your head for the most part

00:08:40.297 --> 00:08:43.291
is simply because you're not
yet appropriately engaged

00:08:43.291 --> 00:08:44.831
with whatever that thing is

00:08:44.831 --> 00:08:47.487
and it's got you as opposed to you got it.

00:08:47.487 --> 00:08:48.662
So interestingly,

00:08:48.662 --> 00:08:49.889
you have to learn the skill

00:08:49.889 --> 00:08:52.135
and what I discovered and developed

00:08:52.135 --> 00:08:53.802
and really recognized,
I didn't make it up,

00:08:53.802 --> 00:08:55.341
this is nothing new.

00:08:55.341 --> 00:08:56.757
- Sure. This is humanity that--

00:08:56.757 --> 00:08:58.891
- Yeah, what I did was make explicit

00:08:58.891 --> 00:09:01.690
what we implicitly do to get things done.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:02.982
But the truth is

00:09:02.982 --> 00:09:05.318
that you actually have to use your mind

00:09:05.318 --> 00:09:07.903
to get stuff off your mind.

00:09:07.903 --> 00:09:09.980
So a lot of people now, "Well,
I want to have a clear head."

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.942
Go "Om." or drink or
something to clear your head.

00:09:12.942 --> 00:09:13.775
- Yeah.

00:09:13.775 --> 00:09:14.705
- As opposed to, wait a minute.

00:09:14.705 --> 00:09:15.946
Why is this on my mind

00:09:15.946 --> 00:09:18.440
and what do I need to do
to get it off my mind?

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.472
And that actually requires thinking.

00:09:20.472 --> 00:09:22.145
- Those are two very important questions.

00:09:22.145 --> 00:09:23.590
- That's bad news for
everybody listening to this.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:27.184
Thinking is required so the
brain can actually think.

00:09:27.184 --> 00:09:29.276
- So let me try and apply that.

00:09:29.276 --> 00:09:31.558
The worldwide audience
sitting in right now,

00:09:31.558 --> 00:09:32.608
is largely creators,

00:09:32.608 --> 00:09:35.397
people who are learning a new skill,

00:09:35.397 --> 00:09:38.430
want to acquire a new skill in photo,

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.513
video design, art, music,

00:09:41.494 --> 00:09:44.166
entrepreneurship, for example.

00:09:44.166 --> 00:09:48.297
And I feel like if I'm going
to be the test monkey here

00:09:48.297 --> 00:09:50.673
and apply what you just said to me

00:09:50.673 --> 00:09:51.673
that when I,

00:09:52.573 --> 00:09:53.815
my best moments

00:09:53.815 --> 00:09:57.140
are when my mind really is the most clear.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:58.317
It's the most sort of empty.

00:09:58.317 --> 00:10:01.097
And I am able to take
on a thing and do it.

00:10:01.097 --> 00:10:02.843
- Either your paints need to be organized

00:10:02.843 --> 00:10:04.025
before you start painting.

00:10:04.025 --> 00:10:06.260
- Right, 'cause then you
know where mustard is.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:09.327
- You need a clear canvas.
- Where cobalt color is.

00:10:09.327 --> 00:10:11.177
- Absolutely, I mean if
you're going to cook,

00:10:11.177 --> 00:10:13.161
how do you want your kitchen to start?

00:10:13.161 --> 00:10:14.580
You've got friends coming over in an hour.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:15.689
- Is that why we clean our room

00:10:15.689 --> 00:10:17.933
before we study for the test?

00:10:17.933 --> 00:10:20.382
- It's why your car drives
better when you get it clean.

00:10:20.382 --> 00:10:21.532
(laughing)

00:10:21.532 --> 00:10:23.868
I coached one very senior executive

00:10:23.868 --> 00:10:25.236
in a very large company

00:10:25.236 --> 00:10:28.653
whose average backlog of email was 4,000.

00:10:30.653 --> 00:10:32.189
His biggest issue was the fact

00:10:32.189 --> 00:10:34.840
that his calendar was booked
for the next two months.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:39.007
Hardly anybody could get
in because of his schedule.

00:10:40.082 --> 00:10:42.357
Why was his calendar booked?

00:10:42.357 --> 00:10:44.454
Because people couldn't get his input

00:10:44.454 --> 00:10:47.779
to be able to okay or get his authority

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:50.606
to move on the things he
had tasked them to do.

00:10:50.606 --> 00:10:52.161
They had to get in his face

00:10:52.161 --> 00:10:53.679
so they needed a meeting.

00:10:53.679 --> 00:10:55.133
After I coached him,

00:10:55.133 --> 00:10:59.156
I've never seen anybody change
that significantly that fast

00:10:59.156 --> 00:11:02.906
but then his backlog
went from 4,000 to zero.

00:11:03.825 --> 00:11:05.565
What happened was,

00:11:05.565 --> 00:11:08.920
he started cleaning up his
backlog and keeping it clean.

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:10.059
Then what happened was

00:11:10.059 --> 00:11:11.910
the pressure came off his calendar.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:12.743
Why?

00:11:12.743 --> 00:11:14.443
'Cause people could get a response

00:11:14.443 --> 00:11:18.919
within 24 to 48 hours by simply
sending the guy an email.

00:11:18.919 --> 00:11:21.258
Huge change in his culture

00:11:21.258 --> 00:11:24.081
simply because of his responsiveness.

00:11:24.081 --> 00:11:27.364
By the way, the sensitivity
of a nervous system

00:11:27.364 --> 00:11:30.823
pretty much indicates where
you are on the food chain.

00:11:30.823 --> 00:11:33.449
So organizations that do not have

00:11:33.449 --> 00:11:35.626
that kind of instant responsiveness

00:11:35.626 --> 00:11:37.818
or people who don't
have that responsiveness

00:11:37.818 --> 00:11:41.208
basically puts you further
down on the food chain.

00:11:41.208 --> 00:11:43.937
Over the years I just found consistency

00:11:43.937 --> 00:11:46.494
because the seeds, boy,
especially in my business,

00:11:46.494 --> 00:11:48.180
seed planting is just amazing.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.013
And it's probably true.

00:11:49.013 --> 00:11:49.846
I mean come on, Stephanie,

00:11:49.846 --> 00:11:52.880
that must be with your business
and anybody's out there.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:53.713
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:11:53.713 --> 00:11:54.804
- With the restaurant, anything else.

00:11:54.804 --> 00:11:56.720
It takes a while to build there

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:58.413
but you just got to stay there

00:11:58.413 --> 00:12:00.331
and you got to keep
doing what you're doing

00:12:00.331 --> 00:12:03.113
and keep putting one foot
in front of the other.

00:12:03.113 --> 00:12:04.683
And that consistency, I think,

00:12:04.683 --> 00:12:06.170
is something that also people,

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:07.412
that resonates with people.

00:12:07.412 --> 00:12:08.627
- [Interviewer] I'm so glad you said that

00:12:08.627 --> 00:12:11.915
because I'm always beating
my drum of consistency.

00:12:11.915 --> 00:12:14.398
Do your marketing consistently
and you will get results.

00:12:14.398 --> 00:12:16.094
And the whole seed planting,

00:12:16.094 --> 00:12:17.996
it's just, I love that message.

00:12:17.996 --> 00:12:21.411
- Yeah, I've had people
come back, boy, years later

00:12:21.411 --> 00:12:23.482
sometimes eight, ten years later.

00:12:23.482 --> 00:12:26.545
"David, I met you at XYZ da-da-da.

00:12:26.545 --> 00:12:29.230
I'm now in this position right now.

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:32.574
You're exactly what we
need and holy mackerel.

00:12:32.574 --> 00:12:34.848
How long did some of these seeds,

00:12:34.848 --> 00:12:36.815
some of them pop right away obviously.

00:12:36.815 --> 00:12:39.081
But you just got to
keep, Johnny Appleseed.

00:12:39.081 --> 00:12:40.946
You just have to keep
spreading the seeds out there

00:12:40.946 --> 00:12:43.422
wherever you can and whatever way you can.

00:12:43.422 --> 00:12:47.110
A good friend of mine, Jack
Canfield, wrote Chicken Soup.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:48.839
Jack's got the great story.

00:12:48.839 --> 00:12:51.014
No publisher would take it.

00:12:51.014 --> 00:12:52.817
He'd fill his trunk with the books,

00:12:52.817 --> 00:12:55.140
and they did go around the
country, just talking about it

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.140
and taking every single radio spot

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:58.314
and interview that they could.

00:12:58.314 --> 00:13:01.605
At some point it reached a critical mass,

00:13:01.605 --> 00:13:03.086
it sort of tipped over.

00:13:03.086 --> 00:13:05.785
And I think there's wisdom in that.

00:13:05.785 --> 00:13:07.801
If you believe in what you're doing

00:13:07.801 --> 00:13:09.186
and you love what you're doing,

00:13:09.186 --> 00:13:12.016
just stick with it and
keep course directing.

00:13:12.016 --> 00:13:14.833
Pay attention to feedback

00:13:14.833 --> 00:13:16.622
and stick to your guns.

00:13:16.622 --> 00:13:18.369
- In your first TED Talk you said

00:13:18.369 --> 00:13:21.286
that the brain is not
necessarily good at multitasking

00:13:21.286 --> 00:13:23.269
but it actually is a good planner

00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:24.310
but it doesn't always know

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:26.094
what to be telling you at the right time.

00:13:26.094 --> 00:13:27.299
For example three in the morning,

00:13:27.299 --> 00:13:28.752
you're thinking about the guy at work

00:13:28.752 --> 00:13:30.185
who you can't do anything about

00:13:30.185 --> 00:13:31.635
but you said that in the long run,

00:13:31.635 --> 00:13:33.476
your mind does plan things.

00:13:33.476 --> 00:13:36.086
It plans reproductive
decisions, eating decisions,

00:13:36.086 --> 00:13:37.713
and things like that.
- Yeah.

00:13:37.713 --> 00:13:39.369
- Can you tell me a bit more about that?

00:13:39.369 --> 00:13:41.061
- Well the mind naturally,

00:13:41.061 --> 00:13:43.314
we naturally do a lot of that stuff

00:13:43.314 --> 00:13:46.844
and all I did was recognized
what the process is

00:13:46.844 --> 00:13:48.963
that we go through when we do that.

00:13:48.963 --> 00:13:51.274
It is kind of interesting,

00:13:51.274 --> 00:13:53.568
I don't know that I have the
solid answer to this yet.

00:13:53.568 --> 00:13:57.107
Why everything I talk about
in the book is easy to do.

00:13:57.107 --> 00:14:00.127
It's stuff we, in a sense naturally do.

00:14:00.127 --> 00:14:02.616
You will naturally plan

00:14:02.616 --> 00:14:04.909
the natural planning model is called that

00:14:04.909 --> 00:14:05.775
because that's how,

00:14:05.775 --> 00:14:07.107
that's how you got dressed,
that's how you walk,

00:14:07.107 --> 00:14:08.072
that's how you talk,

00:14:08.072 --> 00:14:09.846
you have an outcome.

00:14:09.846 --> 00:14:13.239
You have a reason to be
doing what you're doing.

00:14:13.239 --> 00:14:15.614
You have a vision of it being complete

00:14:15.614 --> 00:14:17.021
and that then generates

00:14:17.021 --> 00:14:19.749
all the potentially meaningful
things you need to consider

00:14:19.749 --> 00:14:21.587
and then you sort those and organize them

00:14:21.587 --> 00:14:23.071
and then you take an action.

00:14:23.071 --> 00:14:24.736
So we're doing that all the time

00:14:24.736 --> 00:14:26.230
and yet when people,

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:29.172
they don't really apply that same process

00:14:29.172 --> 00:14:31.697
in much larger or more
complex kinds of things

00:14:31.697 --> 00:14:33.213
in those kind of ways.

00:14:33.213 --> 00:14:34.766
So it's really sort of like learning

00:14:34.766 --> 00:14:35.907
from the smart part of us.

00:14:35.907 --> 00:14:36.740
- Right

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.429
You deconstruct the smart part of us

00:14:38.429 --> 00:14:40.728
and apply that to those longer

00:14:40.728 --> 00:14:42.156
or those other pieces

00:14:42.156 --> 00:14:43.826
that we don't seem to do the smart things.

00:14:43.826 --> 00:14:44.892
- And then that too,

00:14:44.892 --> 00:14:47.456
I just did a tweet 'cause
I was reminded of that

00:14:47.456 --> 00:14:50.822
while I was doing another interview

00:14:50.822 --> 00:14:52.044
that if you,

00:14:52.044 --> 00:14:56.474
you need to make action
decisions when you're smart

00:14:56.474 --> 00:14:59.573
so that when you're dumb
you do smart things.

00:14:59.573 --> 00:15:01.732
Right, 'cause see it's the smart people

00:15:01.732 --> 00:15:03.429
who really realize you're only smart

00:15:03.429 --> 00:15:05.511
and inspired in very random moments.

00:15:05.511 --> 00:15:07.061
So when you're smart and inspired

00:15:07.061 --> 00:15:09.256
you want to use that to
capture the results of that

00:15:09.256 --> 00:15:11.226
and then park the results somewhere,

00:15:11.226 --> 00:15:13.324
where then when you're
kind of thick and stupid

00:15:13.324 --> 00:15:14.711
which is majority of the time,

00:15:14.711 --> 00:15:16.979
you actually do smart
stuff that you figured out

00:15:16.979 --> 00:15:18.318
back when you were smart.

00:15:18.318 --> 00:15:20.521
What happens if you got a whole
list of all the stuff to do

00:15:20.521 --> 00:15:22.231
and you're not doing any of it.

00:15:22.231 --> 00:15:23.064
Relax,

00:15:25.551 --> 00:15:26.884
enjoy, have fun.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.393
Procrastination simply is
not doing a bunch of stuff

00:15:30.393 --> 00:15:32.522
and sometimes the thing
that you need to do

00:15:32.522 --> 00:15:34.410
is stop doing anything.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:35.351
As a matter of fact,

00:15:35.351 --> 00:15:36.403
how good you are

00:15:36.403 --> 00:15:37.939
at this getting things done methodology

00:15:37.939 --> 00:15:41.913
is often indicated by how
well you could do nothing.

00:15:41.913 --> 00:15:44.017
People have a hard time doing nothing.

00:15:44.017 --> 00:15:46.070
But we know now in terms of brain science

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:49.247
that you need as much time
to let your brain relax

00:15:49.247 --> 00:15:52.004
and be spontaneous and daydream

00:15:52.004 --> 00:15:56.417
in order to refresh its
decision-making cognitive power

00:15:56.417 --> 00:15:58.531
'cause you only have a limit of that

00:15:58.531 --> 00:16:00.007
and so if you're not napping,

00:16:00.007 --> 00:16:01.403
if you're not getting enough sleep,

00:16:01.403 --> 00:16:02.788
if you're not backing off

00:16:02.788 --> 00:16:05.842
and letting your mind
just relax and wander

00:16:05.842 --> 00:16:09.929
then you're suboptimizing
your cognitive abilities.

00:16:09.929 --> 00:16:12.370
Yeah, most people procrastinate though

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.129
because they're afraid of
stepping into something

00:16:15.129 --> 00:16:17.366
you don't feel like you
can be successful at.

00:16:17.366 --> 00:16:19.184
So just a simple idea of deciding

00:16:19.184 --> 00:16:21.653
what's the very next
action I need to take.

00:16:21.653 --> 00:16:24.607
Oftentimes people's lists
do not have next actions.

00:16:24.607 --> 00:16:27.669
You'll see things like
on somebody's lists,

00:16:27.669 --> 00:16:29.395
how many of you have something

00:16:29.395 --> 00:16:31.524
that looks like a to-do list somewhere?

00:16:31.524 --> 00:16:32.465
Anybody?

00:16:32.465 --> 00:16:34.897
Yeah 99% of every to-do
list I've ever seen

00:16:34.897 --> 00:16:38.179
is an incomplete list
of still unclear stuff.

00:16:38.179 --> 00:16:41.762
You'll see things on
a to-do list like mom.

00:16:42.729 --> 00:16:44.632
Oh good, well it's good historical data,

00:16:44.632 --> 00:16:45.657
you probably had one.

00:16:45.657 --> 00:16:47.048
Why did you write it down?

00:16:47.048 --> 00:16:48.846
Oh well her birthday's coming.

00:16:48.846 --> 00:16:50.670
Well great, what are you going
to do about her birthday?

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:53.697
I don't know anything about anything.

00:16:53.697 --> 00:16:54.530
Right.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:55.498
In most people's to-do list,

00:16:55.498 --> 00:16:56.331
when you look at them

00:16:56.331 --> 00:16:57.424
still are reminding you

00:16:57.424 --> 00:16:59.282
there's decision-making and thinking

00:16:59.282 --> 00:17:01.321
that you still need to do
about these commitments

00:17:01.321 --> 00:17:03.306
and you don't have the
energy to think and decide.

00:17:03.306 --> 00:17:05.650
So you say stop reminding
me I'm overwhelmed.

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:08.793
So your list become very
unattractive to you.

00:17:08.793 --> 00:17:09.911
So there's lots of reasons

00:17:09.911 --> 00:17:13.411
people may have a high barrier to entry

00:17:13.411 --> 00:17:17.324
to start engaging with the
things that you're committed to.

00:17:17.324 --> 00:17:19.241
Actually, mess is cool.

00:17:20.443 --> 00:17:23.160
Actually the most productive times I am

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.433
is when I have the freedom
to make a creative mess.

00:17:25.433 --> 00:17:27.014
You too.

00:17:27.014 --> 00:17:29.982
You need, I need room to be crazy,

00:17:29.982 --> 00:17:32.627
to make some mistakes, to
brainstorm, to be chaotic.

00:17:32.627 --> 00:17:33.731
Go a little off the edge.

00:17:33.731 --> 00:17:35.775
That is going to be your
most productive time.

00:17:35.775 --> 00:17:37.576
It's when you have that
kind of freedom to do that.

00:17:37.576 --> 00:17:38.551
However folks,

00:17:38.551 --> 00:17:39.513
if you're already in a mess,

00:17:39.513 --> 00:17:41.828
you ain't got room to make one.

00:17:41.828 --> 00:17:43.367
If your kitchen's a mess,

00:17:43.367 --> 00:17:45.214
you don't have time or the energy

00:17:45.214 --> 00:17:47.958
to have a creative
dinner for your friends.

00:17:47.958 --> 00:17:50.656
If your desk and your office are a mess,

00:17:50.656 --> 00:17:53.322
you don't have room and
space to be crazy about

00:17:53.322 --> 00:17:54.952
some new project and spread out

00:17:54.952 --> 00:17:57.970
and have brainstorm with ideas.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:17:59.770
If your email is backed up on you

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:00.909
with a thousand unprocessed emails

00:18:00.909 --> 00:18:03.267
and you've got 3,000 other
things going on in your head,

00:18:03.267 --> 00:18:05.605
you have no space to take
advantage of discretion

00:18:05.605 --> 00:18:07.050
or a time that may show up

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:08.569
in terms of it being creative,

00:18:08.569 --> 00:18:10.569
in terms of your energy.

00:18:14.302 --> 00:18:15.670
The results of that,

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:17.408
if you're trying to use your psyche

00:18:17.408 --> 00:18:19.746
to manage that mess and
you never get out of it,

00:18:19.746 --> 00:18:21.558
is you get the results of two things

00:18:21.558 --> 00:18:23.114
that are the critical elements

00:18:23.114 --> 00:18:25.730
of self and organizational productivity.

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:27.799
You'll lose perspective

00:18:27.799 --> 00:18:30.194
that is you'll lose the
ability to put your focus

00:18:30.194 --> 00:18:32.294
where you need it on exactly
the thing you need it

00:18:32.294 --> 00:18:35.105
at the horizon you need it.

00:18:35.105 --> 00:18:37.819
And or you may be experiencing the results

00:18:37.819 --> 00:18:39.686
of what happens when you lose control,

00:18:39.686 --> 00:18:42.385
That is, I now don't have stability

00:18:42.385 --> 00:18:44.611
and I don't have the freedom in my head

00:18:44.611 --> 00:18:46.759
to be able to put the
appropriate attention

00:18:46.759 --> 00:18:48.717
and execute on it when I do.

00:18:48.717 --> 00:18:51.335
So those are the two key elements.

00:18:51.335 --> 00:18:52.671
Folks, you can't manage time.

00:18:52.671 --> 00:18:53.789
You don't mismanage five minutes

00:18:53.789 --> 00:18:55.382
and come up with six.

00:18:55.382 --> 00:18:57.796
The only time that you think
you need time management

00:18:57.796 --> 00:19:01.411
is when one or both of these
two dynamics is suboptimal.

00:19:01.411 --> 00:19:02.955
Either things need to
be more under control

00:19:02.955 --> 00:19:05.497
or more appropriately focused.

00:19:05.497 --> 00:19:07.559
If you map those two things together,

00:19:07.559 --> 00:19:08.525
what do you get?

00:19:08.525 --> 00:19:10.158
If you're, on the bottom left here,

00:19:10.158 --> 00:19:11.759
we got no control and no focus.

00:19:11.759 --> 00:19:12.599
Ever been there?

00:19:12.599 --> 00:19:15.477
That's your basic victim experience,

00:19:15.477 --> 00:19:17.334
driven by latest and loudest.

00:19:17.334 --> 00:19:18.233
For the most part,

00:19:18.233 --> 00:19:19.821
most of us are thrown there

00:19:19.821 --> 00:19:22.125
by our own over-commitments
and creativity.

00:19:22.125 --> 00:19:25.175
We're just trying to come up for air.

00:19:25.175 --> 00:19:26.943
Now if that part of you shows up

00:19:26.943 --> 00:19:31.130
that has high focus and
perspective but no control

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:33.071
now you're the mad scientist,

00:19:33.071 --> 00:19:34.984
now you're the desperate artiste,

00:19:34.984 --> 00:19:36.347
now you're the crazy maker

00:19:36.347 --> 00:19:37.813
with all kinds of crazy ideas

00:19:37.813 --> 00:19:39.728
and no constraints of what you're doing,

00:19:39.728 --> 00:19:41.118
nothing very well organized.

00:19:41.118 --> 00:19:42.009
Middle of the morning

00:19:42.009 --> 00:19:43.522
you decide to go buy the new iPhone

00:19:43.522 --> 00:19:46.855
but your IT department won't support it.

00:19:47.987 --> 00:19:48.820
On the other hand,

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:50.272
you can get down on the weeds,

00:19:50.272 --> 00:19:53.046
and say oh wait I need to
get 10,000 things organized.

00:19:53.046 --> 00:19:54.276
I need to get all this cleaned up.

00:19:54.276 --> 00:19:56.013
And now you get into micromanagement

00:19:56.013 --> 00:19:57.436
if you don't have appropriate focus

00:19:57.436 --> 00:19:58.526
you can get down on those weeds

00:19:58.526 --> 00:19:59.964
and hung up on them like crazy.

00:19:59.964 --> 00:20:01.274
And you spend a lot of time,

00:20:01.274 --> 00:20:03.356
doing a lot of trying to
get organized about things

00:20:03.356 --> 00:20:05.939
that may not be that important.

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:08.807
In the morning,

00:20:08.807 --> 00:20:10.454
your crazy makers have got a new iPhone,

00:20:10.454 --> 00:20:12.005
in the afternoon you spend two hours

00:20:12.005 --> 00:20:13.211
of what could be a strategic afternoon

00:20:13.211 --> 00:20:16.294
trying to set up the right ringtones.

00:20:18.409 --> 00:20:20.099
Here's what's true about
all three of those, folks.

00:20:20.099 --> 00:20:22.747
You ain't ready for
what's coming towards you.

00:20:22.747 --> 00:20:24.679
You will be suboptimal
in terms of your ability

00:20:24.679 --> 00:20:27.900
to handle the surprises
and they're coming.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:29.071
Good, better, and different.

00:20:29.071 --> 00:20:30.378
They're coming, trust me.

00:20:30.378 --> 00:20:32.830
And you want to be optimally
available for those things

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:34.655
when they come to you.

00:20:34.655 --> 00:20:36.462
It's great to complete things.

00:20:36.462 --> 00:20:38.502
So that's a lot of,

00:20:38.502 --> 00:20:40.428
we know when we finish anything,

00:20:40.428 --> 00:20:43.745
there's a part of us that sort
of reaps energy out of that.

00:20:43.745 --> 00:20:44.839
That feels good.

00:20:44.839 --> 00:20:46.947
Those are your best days when you got,

00:20:46.947 --> 00:20:48.707
you handle that, you turn that in,

00:20:48.707 --> 00:20:50.630
you bought that thing,
you got, you fixed that,

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:52.088
you got the light bulb and screwed it in

00:20:52.088 --> 00:20:53.278
and then it worked, then wow.

00:20:53.278 --> 00:20:54.566
So those are your good days

00:20:54.566 --> 00:20:56.057
when you actually finish a lot of stuff.

00:20:56.057 --> 00:20:59.010
There's a lot of value to
completing an open-loop,

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.319
how and at the same time

00:21:01.319 --> 00:21:03.277
you don't want to feel bad

00:21:03.277 --> 00:21:05.377
if that loop is not closed.

00:21:05.377 --> 00:21:08.064
And that's a lot of what
the GTD methodology does

00:21:08.064 --> 00:21:10.358
is like, look you don't have
to finish all this stuff

00:21:10.358 --> 00:21:11.913
in order to get it off your mind.

00:21:11.913 --> 00:21:13.715
But you do have to identify what it is

00:21:13.715 --> 00:21:16.301
and trust that you'll
see it at the right time

00:21:16.301 --> 00:21:17.812
wherever you are with it,

00:21:17.812 --> 00:21:19.853
at whatever its status is.

00:21:19.853 --> 00:21:22.103
So yeah, you don't want to,

00:21:23.733 --> 00:21:25.670
there's a whole new idea

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:28.354
that goal setting is really
not the best thing to do,

00:21:28.354 --> 00:21:29.871
especially if you set a goal

00:21:29.871 --> 00:21:31.754
and feel bad until you get the goal.

00:21:31.754 --> 00:21:33.019
- Right. Okay.

00:21:33.019 --> 00:21:34.955
- Because then you, then
you're not appreciating

00:21:34.955 --> 00:21:36.519
the process of moving toward it.

00:21:36.519 --> 00:21:38.657
And so you don't want to be able

00:21:38.657 --> 00:21:40.098
to create frustration or guilt

00:21:40.098 --> 00:21:42.310
or undermine your energy

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:45.513
by having some only future ideal win.

00:21:45.513 --> 00:21:49.249
- Is goal setting just
as a pure exercise bad?

00:21:49.249 --> 00:21:51.017
- No, you're setting goals all the time.

00:21:51.017 --> 00:21:52.515
That's how you got
dressed, it's how you talk,

00:21:52.515 --> 00:21:53.602
it's how you walk.

00:21:53.602 --> 00:21:55.479
You're always, you're
seeing yourself out the door

00:21:55.479 --> 00:21:57.154
and then you will fulfill the picture.

00:21:57.154 --> 00:21:58.281
So we're setting,

00:21:58.281 --> 00:21:59.859
you can't stop actually

00:21:59.859 --> 00:22:02.292
because you're a teleological being.

00:22:02.292 --> 00:22:04.301
You're always moving towards something.

00:22:04.301 --> 00:22:05.316
There's a part of us

00:22:05.316 --> 00:22:08.616
that's always attempting to
expand, express, fulfill,

00:22:08.616 --> 00:22:12.565
complete something, just
look, just look around

00:22:12.565 --> 00:22:14.207
just anecdotally anyway

00:22:14.207 --> 00:22:16.085
that everybody's doing something

00:22:16.085 --> 00:22:17.345
even when you're not doing something

00:22:17.345 --> 00:22:18.485
you're doing that.
- Right.

00:22:18.485 --> 00:22:20.408
- So there's an
intentionality still there.

00:22:20.408 --> 00:22:21.473
- But like a goal of

00:22:21.473 --> 00:22:23.095
I'm going to be a
billionaire in three years

00:22:23.095 --> 00:22:25.794
da-da-da might not always be
the most productive thing.

00:22:25.794 --> 00:22:27.459
- It depends on what you're doing with it.

00:22:27.459 --> 00:22:29.605
It depends on whether it's running you

00:22:29.605 --> 00:22:31.465
or you're managing that call.

00:22:31.465 --> 00:22:32.683
Hey here's a picture, here's an image.

00:22:32.683 --> 00:22:34.386
Let me just stick that in there.

00:22:34.386 --> 00:22:35.219
Why not?

00:22:36.269 --> 00:22:38.564
It's better to have nice positive

00:22:38.564 --> 00:22:40.290
pictures and images in your head

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:42.126
than a bunch of negative ones

00:22:42.126 --> 00:22:44.432
and as long as it creates expansion

00:22:44.432 --> 00:22:45.615
instead of contraction

00:22:45.615 --> 00:22:46.720
I think that's the big key.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.654
That's something that I learned years ago

00:22:48.654 --> 00:22:52.343
as a marker for me about
whether things are in alignment

00:22:52.343 --> 00:22:55.533
at whatever level you
want to interpret that,

00:22:55.533 --> 00:23:00.029
is engaging in this creating
expansion or contraction?

00:23:00.029 --> 00:23:01.051
Love or fear?

00:23:01.051 --> 00:23:02.396
So you can look at it that way.

00:23:02.396 --> 00:23:03.229
- [Interviewer] Okay.

00:23:03.229 --> 00:23:04.298
- Same thing. Right?

00:23:04.298 --> 00:23:06.631
In loving, everything works.

00:23:08.224 --> 00:23:10.469
In fear, nothing works.

00:23:10.469 --> 00:23:11.891
So it's in a way,

00:23:11.891 --> 00:23:14.248
it's just that that aspect of making sure.

00:23:14.248 --> 00:23:16.545
So don't beat the medium.

00:23:16.545 --> 00:23:18.263
And don't there's no reason,

00:23:18.263 --> 00:23:20.795
there's no bad or good about
any of these kinds of things,

00:23:20.795 --> 00:23:22.134
whether it's goal setting or not

00:23:22.134 --> 00:23:24.950
other than is that the most optimal thing,

00:23:24.950 --> 00:23:27.268
way to be engaging with it.

00:23:27.268 --> 00:23:29.844
- Okay, and love or fear,
expansion contraction.

00:23:29.844 --> 00:23:32.145
That's kind of a way and
you've got a gut feeling

00:23:32.145 --> 00:23:34.398
whether you're opening
yourself up doing that

00:23:34.398 --> 00:23:36.997
or whether you're protecting and limiting.

00:23:36.997 --> 00:23:39.598
- Yeah and it's also a
good way for decisions,

00:23:39.598 --> 00:23:41.368
like I don't know which way I should go.

00:23:41.368 --> 00:23:43.001
Should I change jobs?

00:23:43.001 --> 00:23:45.512
Should I move to Amsterdam, whatever.

00:23:45.512 --> 00:23:49.819
Start doing, practicing,
rehearsing scenarios in your mind

00:23:49.819 --> 00:23:52.059
about whatever that future scenario is

00:23:52.059 --> 00:23:56.226
and see if that's feeling
expansive or contractive.

00:23:57.337 --> 00:23:58.796
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:23:58.796 --> 00:24:01.983
I made this video because
Eoghan Champkin asked me to.

00:24:01.983 --> 00:24:03.299
I hope I pronounced that okay.

00:24:03.299 --> 00:24:04.454
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:24:04.454 --> 00:24:05.994
that you want me to profile next.

00:24:05.994 --> 00:24:07.232
Leave it down the comments below

00:24:07.232 --> 00:24:08.249
and I'll see what I can do,

00:24:08.249 --> 00:24:09.082
I'd also love to know

00:24:09.082 --> 00:24:10.044
what did David say

00:24:10.044 --> 00:24:11.709
that really left its mark on you?

00:24:11.709 --> 00:24:13.183
What rule did you like the best?

00:24:13.183 --> 00:24:14.660
What clip really inspired you?

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:15.601
Leave it in the comments

00:24:15.601 --> 00:24:17.479
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:24:17.479 --> 00:24:20.445
I also want to give a quick
shadow to Alejandro Baltazar.

00:24:20.445 --> 00:24:21.901
Alejandro, thank you so much

00:24:21.901 --> 00:24:23.826
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word.

00:24:23.826 --> 00:24:25.875
It really really really means a lot to me.

00:24:25.875 --> 00:24:27.247
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:24:27.247 --> 00:24:28.392
I believe in you,

00:24:28.392 --> 00:24:30.277
I hope you continue to
believe in yourselves.

00:24:30.277 --> 00:24:32.372
And whatever your one word is,

00:24:32.372 --> 00:24:33.205
much love.

00:24:33.205 --> 00:24:34.705
I'll see you soon.

00:24:40.827 --> 00:24:42.014
- I think over time

00:24:42.014 --> 00:24:43.963
people develop their own signature

00:24:43.963 --> 00:24:44.958
and I think it takes a while

00:24:44.958 --> 00:24:47.066
to find out what your signature really is.

00:24:47.066 --> 00:24:48.660
So I'll fast forward a little bit

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:49.777
where I am now.

00:24:49.777 --> 00:24:51.699
We never really marketed ourselves

00:24:51.699 --> 00:24:53.224
as I began to build a little company

00:24:53.224 --> 00:24:54.329
around the success of the book

00:24:54.329 --> 00:24:57.457
and brought in other trainers
and coaches and presenters

00:24:57.457 --> 00:24:59.529
and started to build
some product around that.

00:24:59.529 --> 00:25:01.019
Still not a huge company.

00:25:01.019 --> 00:25:04.477
There are only 20 plus
of us doing that work

00:25:04.477 --> 00:25:06.208
but we're partnering with
other people out there.

00:25:06.208 --> 00:25:07.841
But as I started to do that

00:25:07.841 --> 00:25:09.383
we really realized

00:25:09.383 --> 00:25:11.564
that the brand had kind
of run out from under us.

00:25:11.564 --> 00:25:13.717
GTD which was the shorthand
for Getting Things Done

00:25:13.717 --> 00:25:15.033
became this global thing,

00:25:15.033 --> 00:25:16.506
kind of like Kleenex and Xerox.

00:25:16.506 --> 00:25:17.633
And we didn't own that.

00:25:17.633 --> 00:25:18.699
I mean we owned it

00:25:18.699 --> 00:25:20.811
but we, it was kind of
running faster than us

00:25:20.811 --> 00:25:22.352
and we really had to rethink

00:25:22.352 --> 00:25:24.130
how are we showing up
out there in the world

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:26.552
because people would meet me or meet us

00:25:26.552 --> 00:25:29.315
and go wow you're nothing like
what I expected you to be.

00:25:29.315 --> 00:25:30.867
I said well what did you expect me to be?

00:25:30.867 --> 00:25:33.701
They said, well corporate,
left brained, and hardworking

00:25:33.701 --> 00:25:36.191
and rigorous and disciplined and whatever.

00:25:36.191 --> 00:25:37.740
And a lot of people go you know

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:39.312
I probably should but that's not me

00:25:39.312 --> 00:25:41.016
but that's not us either

00:25:41.016 --> 00:25:42.782
and so there was a disconnect

00:25:42.782 --> 00:25:45.342
between how we were actually
showing up in the world

00:25:45.342 --> 00:25:48.288
and people's perception
of us and the reality.

00:25:48.288 --> 00:25:52.126
So we had a really smart guy
help us think through this.

00:25:52.126 --> 00:25:54.236
And it's really about being authentic.

00:25:54.236 --> 00:25:56.735
So those really tie together,

00:25:56.735 --> 00:25:57.925
what's my signature

00:25:57.925 --> 00:25:59.033
what's the David Allen signature?

00:25:59.033 --> 00:26:00.474
If you go to my website now

00:26:00.474 --> 00:26:03.398
if anybody ever saw it a year or two ago

00:26:03.398 --> 00:26:05.823
it looks very different
now than it did then

00:26:05.823 --> 00:26:07.114
because what we decided was,

00:26:07.114 --> 00:26:08.547
look, take the tie off.

00:26:08.547 --> 00:26:10.248
(laughing)

00:26:10.248 --> 00:26:11.990
Because I'm really kind of a hanging out,

00:26:11.990 --> 00:26:13.706
spontaneous kind of guy

00:26:13.706 --> 00:26:16.126
which is really a lot of
what my methodology is about,

00:26:16.126 --> 00:26:17.743
what the message is about.

00:26:17.743 --> 00:26:20.175
But initially we had targeted the market

00:26:20.175 --> 00:26:21.924
those are the people,

00:26:21.924 --> 00:26:24.188
the high profile professionals

00:26:24.188 --> 00:26:25.580
and fast track professionals

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:27.483
were the ones who were
probably in the most pain

00:26:27.483 --> 00:26:28.596
in terms of overwhelm

00:26:28.596 --> 00:26:30.173
and that could use the
methodology the most

00:26:30.173 --> 00:26:31.117
to begin with.

00:26:31.117 --> 00:26:32.667
But the truth is it's for anybody

00:26:32.667 --> 00:26:34.544
and it's now spread around the world

00:26:34.544 --> 00:26:35.889
with our signature.

00:26:35.889 --> 00:26:38.210
In other words, our expression
is much more aligned

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:39.422
with who we really.

00:26:39.422 --> 00:26:41.427
So we didn't really have to go anywhere.

00:26:41.427 --> 00:26:44.044
Many times the problem companies have

00:26:44.044 --> 00:26:45.811
is their marketing is a big "Tada!"

00:26:45.811 --> 00:26:47.995
and they can't deliver on the big "Tada!".

00:26:47.995 --> 00:26:49.484
Well our "Tada!" sucked.

00:26:49.484 --> 00:26:51.655
We were delivering huge value

00:26:51.655 --> 00:26:54.774
and people were having
transformational experiences

00:26:54.774 --> 00:26:57.310
but it didn't look that way.

00:26:57.310 --> 00:26:58.513
So we said well

00:26:58.513 --> 00:27:00.032
there are a lot of
people we're not reaching

00:27:00.032 --> 00:27:01.635
because there's kind of an inauthenticness

00:27:01.635 --> 00:27:03.427
about what we were doing.

00:27:03.427 --> 00:27:04.805
- [Interviewer] So that's been aligned now

00:27:04.805 --> 00:27:06.383
within the website and a brand.

00:27:06.383 --> 00:27:08.896
- Yeah yeah much more, much more so.

00:27:08.896 --> 00:27:09.930
Whether it works as a business,

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:12.231
I don't know but it's
certainly a lot more fun.

00:27:12.231 --> 00:27:13.398
- [Interviewer] Okay.

00:27:13.398 --> 00:27:14.731
- I don't have to show them,

00:27:14.731 --> 00:27:15.934
try to be somebody else

00:27:15.934 --> 00:27:18.160
or have to deal with
people's perception of me

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:19.818
not being who I really am

00:27:19.818 --> 00:27:22.841
and so maybe more
approachable lowering the bar

00:27:22.841 --> 00:27:24.175
for people to be able to engage

00:27:24.175 --> 00:27:25.918
and to engage with me.

00:27:25.918 --> 00:27:27.732
Early on you have to kind of,

00:27:27.732 --> 00:27:30.421
everybody probably gets
a nice business card

00:27:30.421 --> 00:27:32.175
so that you look like you're bigger

00:27:32.175 --> 00:27:33.615
than you probably really are.

00:27:33.615 --> 00:27:35.213
You start to create an image

00:27:35.213 --> 00:27:36.596
and a brand around

00:27:36.596 --> 00:27:39.853
who we think we really ought to be

00:27:39.853 --> 00:27:41.500
so we can attract people

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:44.062
and not turn them off.

00:27:44.062 --> 00:27:45.027
But at some point

00:27:45.027 --> 00:27:46.996
you really need to kind
of catch up with that

00:27:46.996 --> 00:27:49.361
and allow yourself to just
kind of be who you are

00:27:49.361 --> 00:27:50.453
and let the world come to you,

00:27:50.453 --> 00:27:51.930
that are interested in you.

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:53.385
- [Interviewer] Okay, makes sense.

00:27:53.385 --> 00:27:56.003
- But that has been as much
of personal development

00:27:56.003 --> 00:27:59.110
development as a marketing
development on my side.

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:00.398
- [Interviewer] Okay, that's fascinating.

00:28:00.398 --> 00:28:03.174
Another, again there's
two things happening here.

00:28:03.174 --> 00:28:05.792
As you develop in a personal
element, the marketing,

00:28:05.792 --> 00:28:07.075
those two things are aligned

00:28:07.075 --> 00:28:08.590
and they're important for them both

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:11.456
to create a successful business.

00:28:11.456 --> 00:28:14.047
- I think so but I'm not,

00:28:14.047 --> 00:28:16.709
I haven't studied all
the successful businesses

00:28:16.709 --> 00:28:18.488
to say that's how they did it.

00:28:18.488 --> 00:28:20.620
I do know that in the
world that we're in now

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:21.713
because it's so transparent

00:28:21.713 --> 00:28:25.296
and social media can
spread word like crazy

00:28:26.168 --> 00:28:29.703
and you know that
essentially that transparency

00:28:29.703 --> 00:28:31.404
the more authentic you are

00:28:31.404 --> 00:28:33.195
probably the safer it's going to be

00:28:33.195 --> 00:28:35.149
so you don't have to worry about

00:28:35.149 --> 00:28:36.816
trying to be something that you're not.

00:28:36.816 --> 00:28:38.078
Or gee, they followed me at that

00:28:38.078 --> 00:28:39.601
and now I have to be that for them

00:28:39.601 --> 00:28:40.986
and I have to be that for them.

00:28:40.986 --> 00:28:42.153
- [Interviewer] It just makes life easier.

00:28:42.153 --> 00:28:43.884
- It does make life easier.

00:28:43.884 --> 00:28:46.350
Whether it makes it more
profitable I would think so

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:49.326
but yeah I think the world
gets attracted to authenticity.

00:28:49.326 --> 00:28:50.159
- [Interviewer] I agree.

00:28:50.159 --> 00:28:52.867
- And that's I think where the world is

00:28:52.867 --> 00:28:55.597
because it's so transparent.

00:28:55.597 --> 00:28:58.141
Inauthenticity I think shows up

00:28:58.141 --> 00:29:00.872
at least subliminally if not directly.

00:29:00.872 --> 00:29:02.371
So people out there

00:29:02.371 --> 00:29:04.376
who are trying to create
marketing campaigns

00:29:04.376 --> 00:29:05.926
that really aren't who you are

00:29:05.926 --> 00:29:07.293
or trying to show up as something

00:29:07.293 --> 00:29:09.242
that's just somebody's writing ad copy

00:29:09.242 --> 00:29:11.602
but then your engagement with the company

00:29:11.602 --> 00:29:15.872
doesn't hold up to that or
doesn't match with that.

00:29:15.872 --> 00:29:17.514
That's tricky business.

